  "reason": "The records collectively make the later strategy plausibly reconstructible. Multiple studies already combine high-energy or mechanical alloying under argon with short-duration vacuum SPS in multicomponent HEAs, including systematic variation of milling time. R5, R7, R11, and R16 directly connect this route to grain refinement, Hall–Petch strengthening, solid-solution strengthening, and increased hardness or compressive strength. R14 shows milling-driven conversion of elemental powders into a solid solution through severe deformation, while R6 links deformation-generated defects and temperature to FCC/BCC phase evolution in the closely related AlCoCrFeNi system. Other records establish nanocrystalline grain-boundary hardening, lattice-strain strengthening, dislocation-related hardening, rapid SPS densification, and the role of atomic size or melting-point differences in solution and phase formation. The exact CoCrFeNiTi composition, 1–6 h milling window, 900–1000 °C SPS schedule, and 83% FCC/17% BCC fraction are not disclosed, but an expert could plausibly propose the intervention and principal mechanism from the set."
